---
.title = "Why OCapN",
.description = "OCapN held against the protocols you already run. If you know what a bearer token is, this page has all the background it assumes.",
.date = .date("2026-07-25T00:00:00"),
.layout = "why.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

glowcap speaks [OCapN](https://github.com/ocapn/ocapn), a protocol in which
holding a reference to an object is the permission to use it.

# How common protocols authorize

When a program calls a service, it usually identifies itself first. An HTTP
request carries a bearer token, a cookie, or an API key. An SSH client logs in
as an account. An IRC client identifies with a nickname and a password. In
each case the service looks the caller up in a permission table and decides
what to allow.

Security people call this ambient authority: permission comes from who you
are. Once you are authenticated, every line of code running as you holds all
of it, including every library you link.

The model works until you need to hand part of your access to someone else. A
bearer token rarely means one album, read only, for an hour. It usually means
everything your account can do, until someone rotates it, and any program you
give it to can do all of that as you.

# One job, delegated two ways

The steps below hand work to a thumbnailer, first with a bearer token and then
with capabilities.

