		     chip riding the lane; Print joins Thumbnailer's row and the
		     direct edge caps the story. -->
		<g data-s="8">
			<rect x="442" y="240" width="175" height="70" rx="10" fill="var(--paper-raised, #fff9ee)" stroke="var(--rule, #e7d4b7)"/>
			<text x="529" y="280" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="600">Print service</text>
			<path d="M164 167 L237 238" fill="none" stroke="var(--accent, #c76f18)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#cw-arr-a)"/>
			<rect x="145" y="191" width="110" height="22" rx="11" fill="var(--dia-warm-fill, #f7e5d0)" stroke="var(--accent, #c76f18)"/>
			<text x="200" y="206" text-anchor="middle" class="tok">introduction</text>
			<rect x="235" y="299" width="110" height="22" rx="11" fill="var(--dia-cool-fill, #e5eae2)" stroke="var(--teal, #257f88)"/>
			<text x="290" y="314" text-anchor="middle" class="cap">print</text>
			<circle cx="345" cy="310" r="3" fill="var(--teal, #257f88)"/>
			<path d="M345 310 C 380 306, 412 292, 439 277" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal, #257f88)" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#cw-arr-t)"/>
			<text x="470" y="230" text-anchor="middle" class="soft">they talk directly</text>
		</g>
	</svg>
	<div class="capwalk-controls" hidden>
		<button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-dir="-1">Back</button>
		<span class="capwalk-where">Step 1 of 8</span>
		<button type="button" class="btn btn-secondary" data-dir="1">Next</button>
	</div>
	<ol class="capwalk-steps">
		<li>Every request carries a credential, and the credential names you, not the action. The service looks you up in its permission table and decides.</li>
		<li>You want the thumbnailer to shrink one album. Your token is the smallest thing you have to hand it, so the thumbnailer now holds everything your account can do.</li>
		<li>The thumbnailer has a bug, and it deletes your photos. The service allowed it, because every request looked like it came from you. This is the confused deputy problem.</li>
		<li>Under capability rules, signing in returns a live reference to your album. Holding that reference is the permission, and there is no permission table.</li>
		<li>You pass the thumbnailer a reference to that one album. It never learns who you are, and it cannot reach anything else of yours. Passing a reference like this is delegation.</li>
		<li>You would rather it could not delete anything, so you hand it a wrapper instead: an object of yours, running in your process, that forwards reads to the album and refuses the rest. The Photos service never knows the wrapper exists. Narrowing a grant this way is attenuation.</li>
		<li>When the job is done, you switch the wrapper off, and every copy of it stops working. Your own reference still works, and the Photos service is not involved. This is revocation.</li>
		<li>Later the thumbnailer needs your print service, so you send it a signed introduction. It presents the introduction to the print service, and the two talk over their own session from then on. This is a three-party handoff, and the path bypasses you.</li>
	</ol>
</figure>
```

# How a reference travels

A reference still crosses the network as bytes, but the bytes are not the
permission. When two programs meet, each proves a key it holds, and a session
opens between them. Each side keeps a private table of the objects it has
chosen to share, and your album travels as a position in that table, say
slot 3. Slot 3 means your album only inside this one session. The table holds
no identities and no rights, only which object each position means.

A bearer token works for whoever presents it, anywhere, so a leaked token
hands over your access. Someone who records capability traffic gets slot
numbers for a session they cannot join, and guessing a slot number gets them
no further. The transport still has to protect the connection, as it does for
your tokens.

