				<path d="M127.5 94 h8 m-3.5 0 v4" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal-deep, #1b5f66)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
				<circle cx="216" cy="94" r="3.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal-deep, #1b5f66)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
				<path d="M212.5 94 h-8 m3.5 0 v4" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal-deep, #1b5f66)" stroke-width="1.6"/>
				<text x="167" y="98" text-anchor="middle" class="cap">slot 3</text>
				<path d="M190 89 L198 94 L190 99 z" fill="var(--teal, #257f88)"/>
				<rect x="14" y="190" width="110" height="40" rx="10" fill="var(--paper-raised, #fff9ee)" stroke="var(--rule, #e7d4b7)"/>
				<text x="69" y="214" text-anchor="middle">someone else</text>
				<g transform="rotate(-5 160 212)">
					<rect x="132" y="202" width="56" height="20" rx="3" fill="var(--paper, #fff3df)" stroke="var(--dia-muted, #a99d8c)" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
					<text x="160" y="216" text-anchor="middle" class="dead">slot 3</text>
				</g>
				<path d="M192 211 L224 209" fill="none" stroke="var(--dia-muted, #a99d8c)" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>
				<rect x="232" y="190" width="30" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--paper-raised, #fff9ee)" stroke="var(--rule, #e7d4b7)"/>
				<rect x="296" y="190" width="30" height="40" rx="6" fill="var(--paper-raised, #fff9ee)" stroke="var(--rule, #e7d4b7)"/>
				<rect x="262" y="200" width="34" height="20" rx="10" fill="var(--teal, #257f88)" fill-opacity="0.07" stroke="var(--teal, #257f88)"/>
				<circle cx="271" cy="210" r="2.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal-deep, #1b5f66)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
				<circle cx="287" cy="210" r="2.5" fill="none" stroke="var(--teal-deep, #1b5f66)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
				<text x="170" y="258" text-anchor="middle" class="dead">means nothing here</text>
			</svg>
		</div>
	</div>
	<figcaption>What crosses the wire is a position in a table that only the two ends of this session keep.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

You still need a first reference, and you get it the usual way. You sign in
once, or you redeem a durable link called a sturdyref, an unguessable address
that names one published object. After that, access moves by passing
references inside sessions. A sturdyref does work like a bearer secret, but it
names only what its maker chose to publish, never an account.

# Where the idea comes from

Memory-safe languages already work this way inside one process. Code can use
an object only if it was given a reference to it, and it cannot make a pointer
to a private object out of nothing. Object-capability security builds the
whole security model on that rule.

The idea has been around since 1966, when
[a paper](https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/365230.365252) on programs sharing one
machine gave it a name. Operating systems were built on it.
[KeyKOS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/KeyKOS) ran commercial workloads from
1983, [EROS](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/EROS_%28microkernel%29) and
[CapROS](https://github.com/capros-os/capros) rebuilt the design, and
[seL4](https://sel4.systems/) holds the same rule under a machine-checked
proof. The
[E language](https://web.archive.org/web/20241231230559/http://erights.org/)
took it onto a network and produced
[CapTP](https://web.archive.org/web/20250114141521/http://erights.org/elib/distrib/captp/),
along with [vats]($link.page('modules')), containers that run their objects
one call at a time, and [promise pipelining]($link.page('concepts')).

[Sandstorm](https://sandstorm.org/) runs web applications this way, giving every
document its own isolated sandbox. [Agoric](https://agoric.com/) runs financial
contracts on a hardened JavaScript.
[Spritely Goblins](https://spritely.institute/goblins/) brings the idea to
current application languages, and OCapN is the standard
[that community](https://spritely.institute/) is writing so the implementations
can reach each other.

# What OCapN is

OCapN is one message model over a choice of wire encodings and transports, so
the same references travel over encrypted QUIC, a sealed TCP stream, or a
browser DataChannel. Calls return promises you can keep calling through, so a
chain of dependent calls crosses the network once instead of once per call.
glowcap checks that it works with Spritely Goblins over CapTP 1.0, encoded in
[Syrup](https://github.com/ocapn/syrup), OCapN's wire format.

You can watch a three-party introduction in [Rooms]($link.page('rooms')):
choosing **Message *name* directly** gives you a direct reference to that
member, and the exchange bypasses the room.

# Keep going

- [Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains the mechanisms one level down:
  locators and sturdyrefs, pipelining on the wire, the handoff stages, and
  how two peers agree on a wire format.
- [What about identity?]($link.page('identity')) covers what keys cannot
  answer: who a person is, and whether ten thousand visitors are one.
- The [CapTP CLI]($link.page('cli')) ships a REPL with a guided mode: run
  `glowcap repl`, then type `guide`, and it walks these ideas against a live
  session while you type the verbs.
- [Start building]($link.page('start')) walks a client line by line in Zig
  and links the C ABI and every binding.
- The glossary in
  [capabilities](https://github.com/glowcap-org/glowcap/blob/main/docs/capabilities.md)
  gives every term here a one-line definition.
